  "source_dataset": "MIMIC_IV_NOTE",
  "text": "Allergies:\nSulfa (Sulfonamide Antibiotics) / sotalol\n\nChief Complaint:\nHemothorax\n\nHistory of Present Illness:\nMs. ___ is a ___ with PMH of leiomyosarcoma and metastatic \nmelanoma who went for a left subclavian port placement today \nwhich was complicated by placement difficulty leading to \ntermination prior to port placement.\n\nPer surgery signout, she was placed under conscious sedation for \nL subclavian port placement for metastatic melanoma. The wire \nwouldn't travel down the vein properly, and the patient \nexperienced fluctuating heart rates throughout the procedure. \nGiven failure to advance the wire despite several attempts and \nthe heart rate variability, the procedure was terminated. She \nmaintained adequate O2 sats and was HD stable during the \nprocedure. Post-procedure, there was concern for possible venous \nperforation with new R hemothorax on CXR. A CT chest w/o \ncontrast showed a new right moderate pleural effusion containing \nhigh-density material since ___, concerning for acute \nblood products. She was asymptomatic but given concern about an \nintrathoracic bleed she is being transferred to the FICU for \nfurther management. \n \nPer surgery, interventional pulmonology would like to place a \npigtail drain on ___ morning. They state that they will \ncontact IP in the morning and there is nothing for us to do.\n\nOn arrival to the MICU, she states that she has right lower back \npain which is new since her surgery and is worse with movement \nand with breathing. She also reports a chronic cough which has \nworsened since receiving nivolumab as well as new fatigue since \nthat same time. She otherwise has no symptoms and denies any CP, \nSOB, hemoptysis, abdominal pain, N/V, constipation, diarrhea.\n\nBrief Hospital Course:\n___ with PMH of leiomyosarcoma and metastatic melanoma who \npresents to the FICU after a failed port placement on ___ c/b \nlikely right hemothorax\n\n# Hemothorax: Patient developed right pleural effusion \nconcerning for hemothorax following placement of port on ___ as \ndemonsrated on post-procedure chest X-ray. Per procedure report, \nteam had difficulty advancing wire. Hemothorax possibly \ndeveloped ___ to venous perforation in the setting of multiple \nport placement attempts. Of interest, the port was attempted to \nbe placed on the left side of the chest with development of a \nright-sided pleural effusion. CT chest showed right pleural \neffusion with density consistent with blood. Interventional \npulmonology was consulted who placed a right sided chest pigtail \ndrain at the bedside with drainage of 300-400cc of bright red \nblood over the course of hospital day 1. Patient had CBC checks \nQ4Hs with stabilization of post-procedure hemoglobin of \napproximately 10. A second CT chest on ___ showed decrease in \nsize of the patient's hemothorax. She otherwise remained \nhemodynamically stable and was transferred to the floor. Chest \ntube pulled ___. HD and H/H stable. Given persistent O2 \nrequirement a CTA chest was done which was negative for PE but \ndid show persistent small to moderate right pleueral effusion. \nIP was re-consulted and assessed effusion at bedside via \nultrasound but given size and location was not safe to drain at \nbedside. After discussion with IP and Thoracic Surgery, they \nrecommended no further interventions at this time with hope of \nfluid reabsorption and recommended follow-up in \n___ clinic.\n\n# Acute Hypoxic Respiratory Failure: Patient requiring \nsupplement O2 since admission. Desaturation yesterday to mid-80s \non RA while ambulating. Requiring 1L supplemental O2 while at \nrest or exertion however not symptomatic. Likely secondary to \nhemothorax and volume overload in the setting of baseline poor \npulmonary status given untreated sleep apnea, previous left lung \nlobectomy, and large body habitus. She was diuressed with IV \nlasix and given nebs. As she had not been on DVT prophlyaxis in \nthe setting of bleed, a CTA chest was done which was negative \nfor PE but did show persistent right hemorrhagic pleural \neffusion. She was discharged with home oxygen and will follow-up \nwith Pulmonary/Thoracics.\n\n# Atrial Fibrillation with RVR: On diltiazem ER and metoprolol \ntartrate for rate control and warfarin for anticoagulation. Per \npatient report, she has had elevated heart rates over the past \nmonth and is supposed to see her Afib specialist within a week \nor two. She is currently asymptomatic. Diltiazem dose was \nfractionated and reduced to 30mg Q6H given concern for bleed. \nMetoprolol 25mg Q6H was staggered with diltiazem for addtional \nrate control. Anticoagulation held given bleed. She was \ndischarged on her home metoprolol and diltizazem with good rate \ncontrol. Her warfarin was restarted prior to discharge.\n\n# Acute on Chronic Diastolic Heart Failure. Patient with EF >60% \nin ___. Weight increased since admission while Lasix on hold \nduring ICU stay. On transfer from ICU to floor patient weight \n294.1 lb. Per patient, baseline weight about 185 lbs. Patient \ndiuressed with several doses of IV lasix to her dry weight. She \nwas discharged on her home lasix.\n\n# HTN: Pressures currently well-controlled. Held home valsartan \ndue to concern for bleed which was restarted at time of \ndischarge.\n\n# Metastatic Melanoma: As above, diagnosed in ___ \npredominantly in the RLL. Now being treated with single-agent \nnivolumab q2 weeks. Followed by Dr. ___. Single lumen \nport placed by ___ on ___. She will follow-up in clinic on \n___.\n\n# Leiomyosarcoma: Previously resected. Dr. ___ and \nfelt that adjuvant therapy was not indicated. She does have a \nRUL lung lesion which may be a metastatic focus of \nleiomyosarcoma, however it was felt that biopsy was not \nindicated at this time unless it grows in size.\n\n# GERD: Continued ranitidine.\n\n# Delayed Gastric Emptying: Continued metoclopramide 10 mg \nQACHS.\n\n====================\nTransitional Issues:\n====================\n- Please discharged on supplemental O2 likely due to right \nhemorrhagic pleural effusion. Patient with follow-up with \nInterventional Pulmonary and Thoracic Surgery with repeat chest \nx-ray for further management of effusion. Please continue to \nmonitor oxygen saturation and titrate O2 as needed.\n- Patient discharged with oxycodone for pain related to her port \nplacement procedure.\n- Please ensure follow-up with Oncology for immunotherapy.\n- Patient had right IJ Port-A-Cath placed by Interventional \nRadiology on ___.",
